---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/golf/', label: 'Play Golf Solitaire' }}
>
  <p>
    Golf Solitaire is one of the fastest, most approachable games in the patience family. There
    are no foundations to build up and no face-down columns to dig through — every tableau card
    is visible from the start, and your only job is to play them off, one by one, onto a single
    pile. This page is a complete rules reference: what the layout is, exactly which moves are
    legal, and how a hand is won or lost. If you'd rather learn by doing, you can
    <a href="/golf/">play Golf Solitaire</a> in your browser right now.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Golf Solitaire?</h2>
  <p>
    Golf is a single-deck game played with a standard 52-card pack. The name comes from the
    traditional scoring format, in which you play a series of deals as "holes" and add up the
    cards you fail to clear, aiming for the lowest total — just like golf. Each CardHearth deal
    stands on its own: the aim is simply to clear the table. Suits are irrelevant throughout;
    only the rank of each card matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup</h2>
  <p>
    Shuffle the 52-card deck and deal <strong>35 cards face up into seven tableau columns of
    five cards each</strong>. Every card is visible, and only the bottom (lowest) card of each
    column is exposed for play. The next card off the deck is turned face up to start the
    <strong>waste pile</strong> (sometimes called the foundation in Golf), which is where you
    play cards. The remaining <strong>16 cards form the stock</strong>, dealt face down. That
    accounts for all 52 cards: 35 in the tableau, 1 on the waste, and 16 in the stock.
  </p>

  <h2>Objective</h2>
  <p>
    Win by <strong>clearing all seven tableau columns</strong>, moving every one of the 35
    column cards onto the waste pile. Empty all seven columns and the hand is won. The waste
    pile and the stock do not need to be empty — only the tableau matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal moves</h2>
  <p>
    On your turn you have at most two kinds of move:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Play a column card to the waste.</strong> Take the bottom-exposed card of any
      tableau column and place it on top of the waste pile, but only if its rank is
      <strong>exactly one higher or one lower</strong> than the current top card of the waste.
      For example, on a 7 you may play either a 6 or an 8. Suit does not matter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Draw a card from the stock.</strong> When no column card plays — or when you
      simply choose to — turn the top card of the stock face up onto the waste. That card
      becomes the new target, opening up fresh plays.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two limits are worth stating plainly, because they shape every game:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The rank does not wrap.</strong> A King and an Ace are <em>not</em> adjacent. Only
      a Queen plays on a King, and only a 2 plays on an Ace — the chain stops dead at each end.
      This makes Kings the trickiest cards on the table.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The stock does not recycle.</strong> You may turn each of the 16 stock cards exactly
      once. There is no redeal: once the stock is exhausted, those cards are gone for good.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How you win, and how a hand ends</h2>
  <p>
    You <strong>win</strong> the moment the last tableau card leaves the table — all seven
    columns empty. A hand <strong>ends without a win</strong> when two things are true at once:
    no column card can legally be played onto the current waste card, <em>and</em> the stock is
    empty so you cannot draw a new target. At that point the cards still sitting in the columns
    are stranded, and the deal is over. Because there is no recycle, careful sequencing matters:
    every stock card you spend is one you can never get back.
  </p>

  <h2>A note on variations</h2>
  <p>
    Golf has several common house rules. Some versions allow the sequence to
    <strong>wrap around</strong>, so an Ace plays on a King and a King plays on an Ace, which
    makes long chains far easier. Others let you redeal the stock, or change the number of
    columns. <strong>CardHearth's Golf uses the classic, stricter rules</strong>: no wrap-around
    and no recycling. If you've played a wrap-around version elsewhere, expect Kings to feel much
    more dangerous here.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know the rules, the next step is playing them well. Our
    <a href="/guides/golf-solitaire-strategy/">Golf Solitaire strategy guide</a> shows how to
    plan long chains and tame those Kings, and the
    <a href="/guides/solitaire-glossary/">solitaire glossary</a> defines any term you're unsure
    about. When you're ready, <a href="/golf/">play Golf Solitaire</a> — or browse the full
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> for more games.
  </p>
</Article>
